---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import CustomisedBreadCrumbs from "../components/CustomisedBreadCrumbs.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — Rohit Ravi"
  description="Why the neural-data work exists: an unexplained illness, a family member's suffering, and the thread from that problem to myelin, anesthesia, sleep, LibriBrain, and handwriting decoding."
>
  <main class="mx-auto flex w-full max-w-2xl flex-col px-4 py-10">
    <CustomisedBreadCrumbs />
    <h1 class="mt-6 scroll-m-20 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight">About</h1>
    <div class="mt-8 flex flex-col gap-5 text-base leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        I am an undergraduate researcher at NYU's Center for Data Science, part of the Courant
        Institute, studying data science with a minor in philosophy. The work on this site is
        machine learning on neural data. This page is why that work exists.
      </p>
      <p>
        In the first month of my sophomore year an illness started, and for a stretch it did not
        stop. The pain and the vomiting were continuous. Specialists added pieces to a regimen
        that made the episodes less frequent and the condition possible to live with. None of
        them found the cause. They still have not. That gap is what sent me into the nervous
        system, using the same machine-learning coursework I was already in. If an exam finds
        nothing structurally wrong, the failure can still sit somewhere adjacent: a genetic
        factor, a mitochondrial one, or a change downstream of the wiring, in myelination.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two things I tried on my own, outside any protocol I was given, mattered in my own case.
        DHA helped. Palmitoylethanolamide with luteolin helped. I am not recommending either of
        them, and I am not claiming I separated their effect from the rest of what I was taking.
        They are a personal experience. DHA is also reported to help some autistic children.
        That report is what turned a private observation into a question about lipid supply.
      </p>
      <p>
        A relative was suffering in a way I recognized from my own illness. Seeing him is what
        made the question urgent. I am not describing his situation, and nothing on this site is
        a treatment or a comment on his care. The
        <a href="/projects/asd-myelin-timing" class="underline hover:font-bold">myelin project</a>
        is a test of that lipid question against public genetic and imaging data, including the
        parts the data did not support.
      </p>
      <p>
        The same illness is why the anesthesia and sleep work is here. In acute episodes the
        pain has stopped under sedation deep enough to count as anesthesia. I am not naming
        those drugs. The question they raised is a technical one. A nervous system can be driven
        into a reduced-activity state and brought back, and it is not obvious what that state
        shares with sleep, or which parts of the change are reversible. That is what the
        <a href="/projects/anesthesia-pipeline" class="underline hover:font-bold">anesthesia page</a>
        is measuring, on a connectome rather than in a clinic.
      </p>
      <p>
        The current end of the thread is decoding, which is work other people can check.
        <a href="/projects/libribrain-2026" class="underline hover:font-bold">LibriBrain</a>
        asks which of fifty words a person heard, read from the magnetic field of the brain,
        and a large part of that score is available from the timing of the recording alone.
        The other current piece is
        <a href="/projects/handwriting-emg" class="underline hover:font-bold">handwriting from wrist EMG</a>,
        on Meta's released benchmark: their model reproduced at 30.07% character error, then
        15.02% after a personal fine-tune and a language model that were not allowed to see the test.
        Neither project treats the illness. Both are the same habit carried into a public
        measurement: build it, try to break it, and keep the result that survives.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is the hire. The skill is neural data and machine learning. The reason it is
        pointed at nervous systems, and at people who were not given an explanation that
        actually accounted for what was happening to them, is the illness.
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
